.TH CCOL_MEMPOOL_ALLOC_ENTRY 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_mempool_alloc_entry \- allocate an entry from the pool
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cmempool.h>

void *ccol_mempool_alloc_entry(ccol_mempool *mp);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Returns a pointer to an available entry from the pool. If the pool is
exhausted and fallback is enabled, allocates from the heap. Contents are
uninitialized.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
mp   Memory pool to allocate from
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns pointer to allocated entry on success
Returns NULL if pool exhausted and no fallback enabled
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
O(1) complexity
Returned memory is uninitialized
The returned pointer is aligned for any object type, the same guarantee
malloc() makes, so any object that can be stored in heap memory can be
stored in a pool entry
Entry must be freed with ccol_mempool_free_entry()

On a thread-safe pool this draws from a small per-thread cache, reaching
the shared free list only when that cache needs refilling. A pool too
small to maintain one, meaning fewer than 8 elements, takes the pool's
lock on every call instead. See
ccol_mempool_create(3) for how to size a pool, and for the recommendation
to measure a candidate configuration rather than assume one.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
Thread-safe if the pool was created with single_threaded = false.
Concurrent callers do not contend on the common path.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_mempool_calloc_entry (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_free_entry (3),
.BR ccol_mempool_create (3)
